{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_even_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_even_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L80-L96","doc":"**The even Rogers–Szegő tower at `t = −1`** — the closed form\n`H_{2j}(−1;q) = ∏_{i<j}(1 − q^{2i+1})·H_0`, the bar-even (cosh-like) partner of the\nodd-sector collapse `rogers_szego_odd_vanishes_at_neg_one`. Together the two lemmas\ncomplete the q-deformed even/odd parity of the q-oscillator basis: the odd (sinh)\nchannel vanishes, while the even (cosh) channel is the finite `q`-product tower — the\nq-Hermite analogue of the bar-even/bar-odd split of `NaStokesKeystone` /\n`BindingHyperbolicRecurrence`. Pure `q`-formal algebra over a generic field (§7c), no\narchimedean input, no fit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_odd_vanishes_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_odd_vanishes_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L65-L77","doc":"**The odd Rogers–Szegő terms vanish at `t = −1`** (`H_{2j+1}(−1;q) = 0`), given the boundary\nvalue `H_1(−1;q) = 0` (which holds since `H_1(t;q) = 1 + t`). By the two-step decoupling above,\nthe odd-index subsequence is `0` for all `j`. This is the **q-deformed even/odd parity**: the\nq-oscillator analogue of \"`sinh` is the odd channel\" — the odd sector collapses, leaving the even\n(cosh-like) Rogers–Szegő tower, exactly mirroring the bar-even/bar-odd split of `NaStokesKeystone`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_t_neg_one_two_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_t_neg_one_two_step","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L56-L63","doc":"**Even/odd decoupling at `t = −1`.** Setting `t = −1` annihilates the `(1+t)` term, so the\nrecurrence becomes the **two-step** relation `H_{m+2} = (1 − q^{m+1})·H_m` linking same-parity\nindices only. This is the algebraic source of the q-Hermite even/odd parity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_q_one_first_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.rogers_szego_q_one_first_order","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L46-L54","doc":"**`q → 1` collapse to the binomial first-order recurrence.** At `q = 1` the coefficient\n`1 − q^{m+1}` vanishes, so the three-term recurrence degenerates to `H_{m+2} = (1+t)·H_{m+1}` —\nthe recurrence of the ordinary binomial `H_n = (1+t)^n·H_0`, the classical (un-deformed) limit of\nRogers–Szegő. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego.recurrence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego.recurrence","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L44-L44","doc":"The Rogers–Szegő three-term recurrence (with `n = m+1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego.mk","kind":"ctor","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L42-L42","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingQHermiteOscillator.FollowsRogersSzego","kind":"class","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingQHermiteOscillator.lean#L39-L44","doc":"**The Rogers–Szegő / continuous q-Hermite three-term recurrence, as a class.** `H n` is the\n`n`-th polynomial value `H_n(t;q)`; the field is the standard recurrence (shifted to avoid `n−1`):\n`H_{m+2} = (1+t)·H_{m+1} − (1−q^{m+1})·t·H_m`. The q-oscillator bound-state basis recurrence. ","signatureChunk":0}]}